## Scoped interpreter correction

The retained `actual-start-01-failed-before-python.json` records Windows rejecting direct installed-binary process creation with Access is denied. It does not show a Python or driver failure. The retained launcher metadata reports both fresh roots absent and no project source imported. No prepared identity exists to rebind.

The correction replaces only the unusable direct installed-binary selection requirement with the principal's observed launcher/interpreter relationship. Before project imports or destination writes, the driver now authenticates the exact correction and metadata documents, launcher file hash/size, separately readable installed Python file hash/size, native alias metadata using `os.lstat`, and the observed `sys.executable`, `sys._base_executable`, `sys.base_prefix`, and Python version. Constants match the retained actual metadata. The alias is explicitly represented as a zero-byte APPEXECLINK entry, with no content hash and no same-file claim equating it to the readable installed binary or launcher. The changed preflight fails on differing evidence or native interpreter identity.

The launcher remains `C:/WINDOWS/py.EXE`, SHA-256 `7a2dad0ccad20e9ddf1217aa1ca3af9ae12207b4990d17d6a5192c28bba3ea36`, size 783,192. The installed binary remains SHA-256 `ad169f4cb4bfb78c7a5c030a4529c19d6643276778e33994c93e145b6191c3ec`, size 171,744. Native alias metadata remains size 0, attributes 1056, reparse tag 2147483675. These distinct identities and actual native sys values are recorded separately in the report. The driver does not assign to those sys values or alter the environment.

The complete scoped diff was reviewed. AST comparison confirms the only changed existing functions are `authenticate_review_gates`, `preflight_paths_and_inputs`, and `main`; the only added function is the alias metadata reader. Remaining changes are interpreter constants and report text. Git/Docker pins, native renderer behavior, prior I1/M1 corrections, preparation calls, root/input protections, policy and caps, and operational exclusion remain unchanged. Native rendering still uses the actual unchanged `sys.executable` alias and its canonical policy-pinned arguments. The report accurately distinguishes preparation launcher, native rendered executable, and installed binary. No new findings arose from this correction.

